  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.5 z²)/2, steep along x (left-right). It is drawn at half the loss as height. Plain steps scale x by 1 − 2.4 × rate, so swings grow for rates above 0.83.",
    "Each step is −rate × (slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with standard deviation sqrt((64 − b)/(63 b)) on each axis, centred on the true slope. It is exactly zero at b = 64 and comes from the fixed seed, so Reset replays it.",
    "Diverged means the loss went past 4.8 or the ball left the drawn bowl (|x| or |z| over 2). Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim. A run also ends as finished after 40 steps.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 6 times their real length on one shared scale. Steps longer than 0.5 are drawn 3 long, and the readout gives the true length. The arrows are hidden once a run ends.",
    "While running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at speed 1."
  ]
}